VPNs and Online Privacy

When you connect to the internet, your Internet Service Provider (ISP) can see your network requests, which include private information like your IP address and virtual location.
A VPN changes your public IP address and hides your virtual location, preventing others from linking your internet activity to your physical location and personal details.
Data Encryption and Encapsulation

VPNs encrypt your data as it travels across the internet, ensuring confidentiality and making it unreadable to unauthorized parties.
Encapsulation is a process where the VPN service wraps your sensitive data in other data packets, allowing network routers to direct traffic without exposing your personal information.
Secure Tunnels and Protection

VPNs create an encrypted tunnel between your device and the VPN server, making your data inaccessible without a cryptographic key.
This provides significant protection, ensuring your data is encrypted and your IP address and virtual location remain private from malicious actors.
